# evidence/audit-chain.jsonl is a strict append-only event log. Its `_meta`
# row declares the append-only contract, and the codex review bot enforces
# "no deleted rows" on every PR that touches it — the policy is verified
# both by contract and by review.
#
# With 14+ parallel merge-variant PRs each appending one row, default merge
# semantics produced a continuous merge-storm: every advance of `dev`
# re-conflicted ~13 other branches against the trailing-line region. Git's
# built-in `union` driver concatenates both sides of an appended-only hunk
# instead of marking conflict, which is the correct semantic here.
#
# Trade-off acknowledged: if a future change ever DOES need to legitimately
# delete a row (e.g. a hard-redact for compliance), `merge=union` will
# silently keep the deleted row when re-merging across branches. That
# scenario must be done as a single linearised commit on `dev`, not in a
# parallel PR.
#
# Scope is deliberately limited to this one file. `registry/fixuptasks.jsonl`
# was considered but excluded: its history shows in-place row REPLACEMENTS
# (status flips on existing rows) so `union` would silently keep stale +
# updated variants of the same logical record, exactly the failure mode the
# codex reviewer flagged on the initial PR. That exclusion still stands --
# see the structural driver registered for it below (ADR-0626), which is a
# real three-way merge per id rather than a union.
evidence/audit-chain.jsonl merge=union

# Cargo.lock is generated, but it is not append-only: package sections can be added, removed,
# or version-replaced by independent branches. Git's text merge and `merge=union` can silently
# keep stale package entries, so Cargo.lock uses the structural three-way driver in
# tools/cargo-lock-merge-driver-app. The driver merges disjoint [[package]] additions and
# exits 1 on same-package version divergence or removal-vs-edit conflicts.
#
# Whenever it reports a conflict it WRITES diff3 markers carrying every side. Git does not re-run
# its own text merge when a driver exits nonzero — it takes whatever the driver left in `%A` as the
# conflicted working tree — so a driver that abstains hands back `ours` alone, unmarked, with the
# other side's packages absent: a clean-looking file that loses data on a reflexive `git add`. That
# was this driver's behaviour until it was fixed; a real `git merge` produced a merge commit
# byte-identical to `ours`. Where it cannot write `%A` at all it exits >128 instead, which git
# treats as a crash and abandons the merge — never a conflict it made no promises about.
#
# Registration is per-clone (see the crate README): an actor without the `merge.cargo-lock` git
# config simply gets today's normal conflict. Extra placeholders after `%O %A %B` (`%P`, `%L`, …)
# are accepted and ignored, so a `%O %A %B %P` registration — the form `merge.faces` uses — is
# safe; demanding exactly three was itself a data-loss bug.
Cargo.lock merge=cargo-lock
# The fixup registry is append-mostly JSONL: line 1 is a schema header with NO `id`, every other
# line is one task row keyed by `id`. Two lanes each filing a finding append adjacent bytes, so
# git's text merge conflicts on essentially every pair of concurrent PRs (GH #1412) over a change
# that is semantically disjoint. `merge=union` is WRONG here for the reason recorded above (in-place
# row replacements would keep both the stale and updated variant of one logical record, and every
# consumer keys on `id`). The structural driver in tools/fixup-ledger-merge-driver-app does a real
# three-way merge per id: it resolves only what is unambiguous, CONFLICTS when both sides edited one
# id differently, and never lets a deletion win. It fails closed on unmodelled input, so git falls
# back to a normal conflict rather than the driver writing a partially-merged ledger. On conflict it
# WRITES diff3 markers carrying every side: git does not re-run its own text merge when a driver
# exits nonzero, so a driver that abstains leaves `ours` alone, unmarked, with the other side's rows
# absent -- a clean-looking file that loses data on a reflexive `git add`.
#
# Registration is per-clone (see the crate README): an actor without the `merge.fixup-ledger` git
# config simply gets today's normal conflict, so this can never make the status quo worse.
#
# Critically, the header is bound to line 1 STRUCTURALLY and never looked up by id -- an id-keyed
# resolver skips it (its id is absent, and `None` is falsy), which silently deleted the header from
# four branches in one session. The driver asserts the header survived before emitting anything.
registry/fixuptasks.jsonl merge=fixup-ledger

# The friction ledger is an append-only, event-sourced JSONL surface with id-aware semantics
# (PRIMARY rows anchor a friction id; UPDATE rows append transitions; the ADR-0544
# friction-accounting gate folds them per id). Plain `merge=union` is WRONG here: it keeps both
# sides of two lanes' PRIMARY rows for one id (friction_duplicate_primary_row, FRIC-1781370000
# incident 1) and duplicates byte-divergent serializations of one logical row (incident 3). The
# structural driver in tools/friction-ledger-merge-driver-app performs an id-aware union with
# second-author primary->update conversion, parsed-JSON logical dedup, and canonical output; it
# exits nonzero on any unparseable/unmodeled side so git falls back to a normal conflict
# (incident 2 — never write garbage). Same deletion trade-off as audit-chain above: base rows are
# preserved, a legitimate redact must be a single linearised commit on `dev`.
# Registration is per-clone (see the crate README): actors without the `merge.friction-ledger`
# git config get a normal conflict; cloud-ci gates remain the canonical backstop.
ci/facade/action-item-accounting/friction-ledger.jsonl merge=friction-ledger

# The admission crate root is a re-export hub. Every module a lane adds needs one
# `pub mod` line and one `pub use` group here, `occupancy (path-set)` claims a whole
# file with no ordering between claimants, and a refused lane cannot enter the merge
# queue -- so two lanes adding unrelated modules refused each other at spawn and the
# only exit was closing one pull request. That is the `Cargo.lock` wedge recorded
# above, on a hand-written file.
#
# `merge=union` is WRONG here, for the reason recorded above. The re-export groups are
# rustfmt-wrapped and edited IN PLACE: two lanes that rewrap one `pub use` group would
# keep both copies, and a duplicated `//!` line would survive with nothing to catch it.
#
# No driver is registered for this name and none is intended. Git falls back to its
# ordinary three-way text merge, which is the correct reconciler here: disjoint module
# additions merge clean, and two lanes editing one region conflict AT MERGE, loudly,
# instead of refusing both lanes at spawn. A bad text merge cannot pass silently --
# a duplicated `pub mod` is E0428, a duplicated re-export is E0252, and a mis-wrapped
# group fails `cargo fmt --check`. What this declaration buys is that the conflict is
# raised where it can be resolved rather than predicted where it usually is not.
pipeline/core/admission/src/lib.rs merge=concurrent-lanes

# Force LF for Rust sources so include_bytes! / git-blob identity stays platform-stable.
# Hosted windows-latest defaults to core.autocrlf=true; CRLF worktree + LF blobs broke
# corpus-doc-parser census ParserSource equality (soft platform-smoke scm-facts integration).
*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
